PRE-REGISTRATION -- zakcode determinism under POD LOAD          2026-09-12
Written BEFORE any zakcode determinism run on 02-median-bug.

WHAT IS ALREADY KNOWN AT THIS MOMENT (stated so nothing here reads as fresh)
  - Claude Code, 02-median-bug, N=3, as it ships: 3/3 PASS, turns [7,6,6],
    TWO distinct output states -- runs 1+2 identical, run 3 a different
    stats.py. Recorded in bench/results/determinism-claude-code-02-median-bug.json.
  - zakcode, m05 at cap=64, N=3, WITH ZBENCH_PIN_IDENTITY: byte-identical 3/3
    (ADR-0152). DIFFERENT TASK. That is the confound this run closes.

THE NEW QUESTION, and why it is not a caveat
  The pod is currently serving the TOPK eval. A vLLM server batches concurrent
  requests, and batch COMPOSITION changes which kernels run; that perturbs
  floating-point reduction order, which can flip an argmax even at temperature
  0 with a byte-identical prompt. So "zakcode reproduces exactly" may be a
  property of a QUIET pod rather than of the loop.
  This is worth measuring rather than avoiding: a determinism claim that holds
  only on an idle server is much weaker than one that survives contention, and
  a user running a shared local model is ALWAYS in the loaded case. It is also
  a question Claude Code's users cannot ask at all -- they do not own the
  server -- so it is native to zakcode's actual deployment story.

DESIGN
  Batch A -- 3 runs of 02-median-bug with ZBENCH_PIN_IDENTITY=1 while the TOPK
             eval is concurrently hitting the same pod (LOADED).
  Batch B -- the identical 3 runs after TOPK exits (QUIET).
  Same task, same pin, same digest function as the reference arm
  (_digest_tree in bench/determinism_arm.py) -- one instrument, both arms, so a
  difference between arms cannot be an artifact of two different measurements.

PREDICTIONS, fixed before data
  P4 Batch B (QUIET) will be byte-identical 3/3. This is the ADR-0152 result
     carried to a new task; if it fails, ADR-0152's claim does not generalise
     across tasks and that is the finding.
  P5 Batch A (LOADED) will ALSO be byte-identical 3/3. I am predicting the
     batching effect does NOT surface here, because 02-median-bug is a short
     task with few tokens of divergence opportunity. Stated as the prediction
     I expect to be RIGHT, which is exactly why the next line matters.
  P6 If A and B differ, the difference is LOAD, not the loop -- and the
     determinism claim must be stated as conditional on server quiescence.

DECISION RULES
  A identical AND B identical -> zakcode reproduces byte-for-byte on this task
     under BOTH conditions. Combined with the Claude Code result above, the
     claim "zakcode with pinning ON is more deterministic than the reference
     agent as it ships" is supported ON THIS TASK at N=3 per arm. It stays a
     claim about an AVAILABLE CAPABILITY vs A SHIPPED DEFAULT, never about two
     loops under identical treatment -- no pinning equivalent exists to apply
     to Claude Code.
  B identical, A not -> the determinism feature is real but LOAD-CONDITIONAL.
     Report it that way, prominently, and do not let the quiet-pod number
     travel without the loaded one. This is the outcome most likely to be
     quietly dropped, so it is named here first.
  B not identical -> ADR-0152 does not generalise to this task. The zakcode
     determinism claim is then TASK-SPECIFIC and must be re-scoped; do not
     rescue it by switching tasks until one reproduces.
  EITHER batch failing to PASS 3/3 -> report the pass rate separately; a
     correctness failure is not a determinism result and must not be scored
     as one.

WHAT N=3 CANNOT DO
  N=3 per batch cannot estimate a RATE of nondeterminism. It can demonstrate
  EXISTENCE (one differing run proves it) and it can fail to demonstrate it.
  "3/3 identical" is not evidence of a zero rate. No rate will be claimed.
